{
  "subquestions": [
    {
      "text": "How does paging the KV cache reduce memory fragmentation in LLM serving?"
    },
    {
      "text": "What is the impact of KV cache paging on throughput and latency in LLM inference?"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "paper_id": "2309.06180",
      "quote": "To address this problem, we propose PagedAttention, an attention algorithm inspired by the classical virtual memory and paging techniques in operating systems. On top of it, we build vLLM, an LLM serving system that achieves (1) near-zero waste in KV cache memory and (2) flexible sharing of KV cache within and across requests to further reduce memory usage.",
      "claim": "PagedAttention reduces memory fragmentation in LLM serving by applying virtual memory and paging techniques to achieve near-zero waste in KV cache memory."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2608.13263",
      "quote": "PagedAttention uses fixed-size memory blocks to reduce allocator-level fragmentation",
      "claim": "PagedAttention reduces memory fragmentation by utilizing fixed-size memory blocks for KV cache management."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2309.06180",
      "quote": "Our evaluations show that vLLM improves the throughput of popular LLMs by 2-4$\\times$ with the same level of latency compared to the state-of-the-art systems, such as FasterTransformer and Orca.",
      "claim": "PagedAttention-based systems like vLLM improve inference throughput by 2-4x while maintaining the same level of latency."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2605.09735",
      "quote": "On a 2-GPU NVIDIA A100 node, KV-RM improves mixed-length decoding throughput and tail latency relative to a static-graph baseline, reduces reserved KV memory across workload families, and removes severe burst-time latency spikes under production-trace replay.",
      "claim": "Paged KV management improves throughput and reduces tail latency in LLM inference."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "takeaway": "Paging the KV cache improves throughput by eliminating memory fragmentation and enabling larger batch sizes through efficient, virtualized memory management {p:2309.06180, p:2605.09735}.",
  "paragraphs": [
    "Paged memory management addresses the memory bottleneck in LLM serving by applying virtual memory techniques to the KV cache, which significantly reduces fragmentation and memory waste {p:2309.06180, p:2608.13263}. By utilizing fixed-size memory blocks, systems can decouple logical KV histories from physical storage, allowing for more flexible memory allocation {p:2605.09735, p:2608.13263}.",
    "This efficient management allows for higher request batching, which directly translates to improved inference throughput {p:2309.06180, p:2605.09735}. Systems implementing these techniques have demonstrated 2-4x throughput gains compared to traditional static-graph baselines while maintaining consistent latency {p:2309.06180, p:2605.09735}."
  ],
  "papers": [
    {
      "paper_id": "2309.06180",
      "contribution_quote": "To address this problem, we propose PagedAttention, an attention algorithm inspired by the classical virtual memory and paging techniques in operating systems. On top of it, we build vLLM, an LLM serving system that achieves (1) near-zero waste in KV cache memory and (2) flexible sharing of KV cache within and across requests to further reduce memory usage."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2608.13263",
      "contribution_quote": "PagedAttention uses fixed-size memory blocks to reduce allocator-level fragmentation"
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2605.09735",
      "contribution_quote": "We present KV-RM, a runtime design that regularizes KV-cache movement beneath a static-graph LLM decoder. KV-RM decouples logical KV histories from physical storage, tracks active KV state through a block pager, and materializes each decode step through a single committed descriptor."
    }
  ]
}

