{
  "takeaway": "Paging the KV cache improves LLM serving throughput by eliminating memory fragmentation and enabling higher batch sizes through efficient, non-contiguous memory allocation {{p:2309.06180}} {{p:2608.13263}} {{p:2605.09735}}.",
  "paragraphs": [
    "By applying virtual memory and paging techniques to KV cache management, systems can achieve near-zero memory waste and support flexible sharing of cache across requests {{p:2309.06180}}. This approach utilizes fixed-size memory blocks to mitigate allocator-level fragmentation, which otherwise limits the number of concurrent requests a system can handle {{p:2608.13263}}.",
    "Decoupling logical KV histories from physical storage allows runtimes to absorb the irregular memory behavior inherent in online decoding {{p:2605.09735}}. Consequently, this optimization enables significant throughput gains, often reaching 2-4x higher performance compared to traditional static-graph or non-paged serving systems {{p:2309.06180}} {{p:2605.09735}}."
  ],
  "papers": [
    {
      "paper_id": "2309.06180",
      "contribution_quote": "To address this problem, we propose PagedAttention, an attention algorithm inspired by the classical virtual memory and paging techniques in operating systems. On top of it, we build vLLM, an LLM serving system that achieves (1) near-zero waste in KV cache memory and (2) flexible sharing of KV cache within and across requests to further reduce memory usage."
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2608.13263",
      "contribution_quote": "PagedAttention uses fixed-size memory blocks to reduce allocator-level fragmentation"
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2605.09735",
      "contribution_quote": "We present KV-RM, a runtime design that regularizes KV-cache movement beneath a static-graph LLM decoder. KV-RM decouples logical KV histories from physical storage, tracks active KV state through a block pager, and materializes each decode step through a single committed descriptor."
    }
  ]
